  "title": "Nouns, verbs, pronouns, articles and adjectives",
  "lesson": {
    "kind": "deepen",
    "focusPoint": 1,
    "points": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "Point 1: Review of the last class",
        "body": "What did you learn in the last class?\n\nLast class you met the nine groups. Noun names, verb acts, pronoun replaces. Today we see them closer on Mount Ávila."
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "heading": "Point 2: Common and proper nouns",
        "body": "“Mountain” and “Mount Ávila” name the same kind of thing. Do they look alike?\n\n“Mountain” is a common noun: it names any mountain. “Mount Ávila” is a proper noun: it names one place and starts with capitals."
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "heading": "Point 3: One or many",
        "body": "One hiker is on Mount Ávila. Five more arrive. How do you say it for many?\n\nOne hiker, five hikers. Add -s: hikers. Some nouns change: one child, two children."
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "heading": "Point 4: The verb can change",
        "body": "Say: “I climb.” Now say it for Sofia. What changes in the verb?\n\nYou say: I climb, you climb, she climbs. With he, she or it, add -s. The word “climb” stays inside."
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "heading": "Point 5: A pronoun avoids repeating",
        "body": "“Sofia climbs. Sofia sees the top.” It sounds repeated. How can you fix it?\n\nSay: “Sofia climbs. She sees the top.” “She” takes the place of Sofia. I, you, he, she, it, we, they: pronouns."
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "heading": "Point 6: Articles and adjectives",
        "body": "Say “a tall hiker” and “an old hiker”. Which words are articles? Which describe?\n\n“A” and “an” are articles (artículos). We say “an old hiker”, “a tall hiker”. “Old” and “tall” are adjectives: they describe, and they never change."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "A noun names, a verb acts, a pronoun replaces, and articles and adjectives go with a noun.",
    "memoryPhrase": "Noun names, verb acts, pronoun replaces.",
    "weekRecap": "Spanish families: -ar (cantar), -er (comer), -ir (vivir). Conjugate for yo, tú, and él in the present.",
